ologically from the  very earliest intersections of travel  and philosophy and then moves forward in  time so it looks for example it and  Henri through has lots to say about the  notion of wilderness in his book Walden  I look at the notion of maps like what a  map is what philosophy can tell us about  that and then ethics is just one of the  many issues facing philosophers today so  if you were to take three things that  philosophy tells us about travel what  would they be in terms of like what we  should or shouldn't do when we when we  travel or why wait that's really nice  okay so I think philosophy definitely  tells us that travel can be a source of  knowledge that it can be mind expanding  lots of philosophers have actually  compared going on a philosophical  journey to going on a literal journey to  distant lands I think what they're  getting out there is that we need to  expand our minds that that's a really  good thing for us to do I also think  that thinking about philosophy can help  us travel more mindfully it can help us  understand the cultural and ethical  significance of the kind of journeys  that we're going on and why we're doing  them in the first place and finally I  think that philosophy can tell us I'm  expected think about why we travel I  snow for example up until the early 18th  century people didn't visit mountains  people weren't very interested in  mountains they thought that they were  ugly warts pop marks upon the earth  there was a sea change in the way that  philosophy thought about landscapes and  that seems to have led to a massive  upset in mountain tourism what  originally changed their mind about was  the nature  space right so in this 17th century you  have a theory becoming really popular  and it's actually a theory that I will  be talking about here and it was held by  Isaac Newton and it's the V that space  is a real kind of thing it's a thing  that has a topology and properties of  its own  Newton identified space with God 