gan asking those questions  I think there are a few different things  that happened one was the view that time  is an unreal illusion became  phenomenally popular partly through the  work of people like Kant and Hegel that  it was really picked up in the  anglo-american world and everyone just  began denying the reality of time and so  they really believe that at the  fundamental level it then did not happen  before or after one another and I think  there was a massive reaction to this  that a whole other group of philosophers  suddenly began saying but that's crazy  of course things happened before and  after one another what's more we feel  like we're in time and then you get this  hodgepodge of science is feeding into  the philosophy so psychology is becoming  really popular for the first time I mean  it's really being developed as a  discipline there's lots of interesting  work going on in physics this is before  relativity so things like Minkowski  saying that space and time should be  unified as a four-dimensional space-time  there's also the biological theory of  evolution which took some time after  Darwin to filter through into philosophy  the in Darwin we have scientists  seeming to say if you give time long  enough it can  a new species it can build mountains and  I think that philosophers really began  to take this stuff on board they really  began to think of time as a kind of  powerful force and then that led them to  to stress this notion that time really  flows it really passes and then you  start getting the debates going between  the philosophers on the one hand you say  time does not flow or pass and  philosophers on the other hand to is  saying it definitely flows and passes  the other subject that you've focused on  even women on metaphysics I was  wondering whether you could bring one or  two examples of female philosophers and  if you discovered and admire and run us  through their ideas very quickly so up  until 20 or 30 years ago people woul